---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatCssUnits from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatCssUnits.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="px-to-em">
  <DevFormatCssUnits client:load from="px" to="em" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What em is relative to</h2>
    <p>
      For every property except one, <code>em</code> resolves against the computed
      <code>font-size</code> of the element itself. For <code>font-size</code>, it resolves against the
      <em>parent&rsquo;s</em> font size. That single exception is the source of nearly all em confusion,
      and it is worth reading twice.
    </p>
    <pre><code>.card &#123;
  font-size: 1.25em;   /* 1.25 × the PARENT's size */
  padding: 1em;        /* 1 × THIS element's size, i.e. 1.25 × parent */
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Set the parent font size field above to the computed size of the containing element (read it from
      the Computed panel in devtools), and the px to em conversion matches what the browser does.
    </p>

    <h2>The compounding trap</h2>
    <p>
      em multiplies down the tree, and inherited rules multiply with it.
    </p>
    <pre><code>ul &#123; font-size: 0.9em; &#125;

Level 1: 16px × 0.9 = 14.4px
Level 2: 14.4 × 0.9 = 12.96px
Level 3: 12.96 × 0.9 = 11.66px
Level 4: 11.66 × 0.9 = 10.5px   ← unreadable</code></pre>
    <p>
      Nothing here is a bug; it is em working exactly as specified. The fix is either to scope the rule
      so it cannot nest (<code>:not(ul ul)</code>, or a class rather than an element selector) or to use
      rem, which is immune because it always resolves against the root.
    </p>

    <h2>Where em is the right choice</h2>
    <p>
      Component-internal measurements that should track the component&rsquo;s own text size. Define a
      button once in em and it works at every size, because everything inside it scales together:
    </p>
    <pre><code>.button &#123;
  font-size: 1rem;        /* the one dial */
  padding: 0.625em 1em;   /* 10px / 16px at this size */
  border-radius: 0.375em;
  gap: 0.5em;
&#125;

.button--sm &#123; font-size: 0.875rem; &#125;  /* everything shrinks proportionally */
.button--lg &#123; font-size: 1.125rem; &#125;  /* everything grows proportionally */</code></pre>
    <p>
      Two size modifiers, one declaration each. In rem you would have to restate the padding, radius and
      gap for every size.
    </p>

    <h2>Other good uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Icon sizing. <code>width: 1em; height: 1em</code> makes an inline SVG match the surrounding text exactly, at any size.</li>
      <li>Media queries. Inside a media query em resolves against the browser&rsquo;s initial font size, never your declared root, which makes em breakpoints more predictable than rem ones in a project that changes the root size.</li>
      <li><strong>Text indentation and hanging punctuation</strong>, which should be proportional to the type by definition.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The bulk rewrite cannot know your tree</h2>
    <p>
      When you paste a stylesheet, the converter applies the single parent font size you set at the top
      to every value it rewrites. It has no way to know that <code>.card p</code> sits inside something
      that set 1.25em, because that depends on the DOM, not the stylesheet. For a file with several nested type
      contexts, convert one section at a time with the right parent size for each, or use the property
      filter to limit the rewrite to a single property.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
